    "global": {
      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "With both coefficients free, a unique finite MLE exists for a nonconstant nonnegative sample exactly when $\\overline{x^2}<2\\bar x^2$. Otherwise a nonconstant sample selects the excluded exponential face. Constant samples have unbounded likelihood. With $a$ held and a positive sample sum, a unique finite estimate of $b$ exists exactly when $\\sum_i x_i>a\\sum_i x_i^2/2$. Otherwise the supremum is at $b=0$. With $a$ held and all observations zero, every positive $b$ is a maximizer. With $b$ held, a unique finite estimate of $a$ exists exactly when the sample sum is positive. Holding both leaves only likelihood evaluation.",
      "reason": "Write $I(b)=\\sum_i\\int_0^{x_i}e^{bt}\\,dt$. Profiling $a$ gives $\\hat a=n/I(b)$ and, up to constants, $b\\sum_i x_i-n\\log I(b)$. The logarithm of this moment-generating integral is strictly convex for positive sample sum. The profile derivative at zero is $n(\\bar x-\\overline{x^2}/(2\\bar x))$, and its limit at infinity is $n(\\bar x-\\max_i x_i)<0$ for nonconstant data. With $a$ held, the second derivative in $b$ is $-a\\sum_i\\int_0^{x_i}t^2e^{bt}\\,dt<0$ and the score at zero is $\\sum_i x_i-a\\sum_i x_i^2/2$. All-zero data instead give likelihood $a^n$, independent of $b$."
